{
  "abstract": "Weight Maximization assigns stochastic units credit from outgoing weight changes. Its trace extension removes the same-example backward phase but introduces delayed and interfering learning signals. We adapt that principle to the supplied 50.3-million-parameter transformer using forward-written action records, old receiver-update packets and finite forward observations of nonlinear interfaces. A residual credit graph bounds delivery age independently of depth; it changes the credit objective and does not inherit the original alignment theorem. Across 39 completed primary runs and eight auxiliary panels, current receiver weights improve development bits per byte over origin snapshots by 0.11961 and 0.12657 in two short-run seeds, and by 0.11872 in a 33-tick local graph. The advantage survives increased depth and width at fixed eight-position sampling, but absolute quality shows little capacity gain. At 499.65 million presented targets the native live/snapshot difference shrinks to 0.01930. Equal-effort target/rest terminal stratification improves short-run full-model loss by 0.11408 and 0.11228, mostly through lexical learning; at full budget its hidden-learning interaction reverses, leaving only 0.00630 BPB over the lexical control. Adding an old MLP-expansion utility with a matched delay control provides no improvement in one seed. We audit every forward lane, probe, packet dependency and actual cost. The best full-budget candidate scores 2.06373 BPB versus 1.65870 for separate sparse-label backpropagation and 0.99963 for the strong dense baseline, without a measured speed advantage. The contribution is an implemented learning mechanism with no local or global backward computation, replicated internal gains and consequential limits; statistical scalability and competitive quality–compute remain unresolved.",
